==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sed-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|residue}}, from {{der|en|fro|residu}}, from {{der|en|la|residuum}}, neuter of {{m|la|residuus||remaining}}, from {{m|la|resideō||I remain behind}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛzɪduː/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-residue.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛzɪdjuː/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Whatever [[remain]]s after something else has been [[remove]]d.
# {{lb|en|chemistry}} The [[substance]] that remains after [[evaporation]], [[distillation]], [[filtration]] or any similar process.
# {{lb|en|biochemistry}} A [[molecule]] that is released from a [[polymer]] after [[bond]]s between neighbouring [[monomer]]s are broken, such as an [[amino acid]] in a [[polypeptide]] chain.
# {{lb|en|legal}} Whatever [[property]] or [[effect]]s are left in an [[estate]] after payment of all debts, other charges and deduction of what is specifically [[bequeathed]] by the [[testator]].
# {{lb|en|complex analysis}} A form of [[complex number]], [[proportional]] to the [[contour integral]] of a [[meromorphic]] [[function]] along a [[path]] enclosing one of its [[singularity|singularities]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2001|title=Publications of the Research Institute for Mathematical Sciences|page=72
|passage=By taking '''residues''', we can represent this integral in terms of a Jackson integral.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|whatever remains}} {{l|en|lave}}, {{l|en|remnant}}; See also [[Thesaurus:remainder]]

